    {
      "n": 1,
      "title": "Aerobic vs Anaerobic Respiration",
      "competency": "STEM_BIO11/12-IIa-j-6",
      "content": "<h3>🎯 Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Differentiate aerobic from anaerobic respiration.</li><li>Explain the role of oxygen.</li><li>Describe fermentation types.</li><li>Compare ATP yield.</li></ul><h3>🔑 Key Concepts</h3><ul><li><strong>Aerobic respiration</strong> — requires oxygen; produces ~36–38 ATP per glucose.</li><li><strong>Anaerobic respiration</strong> — no oxygen; produces 2 ATP per glucose.</li><li><strong>Fermentation</strong> — anaerobic pathway that regenerates NAD⁺.</li></ul><h3>📚 Lesson Content</h3><h4>A. Aerobic vs Anaerobic</h4><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Aerobic</th><th>Anaerobic</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Oxygen</td><td>Required</td><td>Not required</td></tr><tr><td>ATP yield</td><td>~36–38</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Cytoplasm + mitochondria</td><td>Cytoplasm</td></tr><tr><td>Products</td><td>CO₂ + H₂O</td><td>Lactic acid or ethanol + CO₂</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>B. Types of Fermentation</h4><ul><li><strong>Lactic acid fermentation</strong> — muscle cells (produces lactate)</li><li><strong>Alcoholic fermentation</strong> — yeast (produces ethanol + CO₂)</li></ul>",
      "activity1": {
        "timeLimit": 90,
        "badgeId": "aerobic-anaerobic",
