      "title": "Krebs Cycle",
      "competency": "STEM_BIO11/12-IIa-j-7, -8",
      "content": "<h3>🎯 Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Describe the Krebs cycle.</li><li>Trace the fate of pyruvate.</li><li>Identify the products of the Krebs cycle.</li><li>Explain the role of the Krebs cycle in metabolism.</li></ul><h3>🔑 Key Concepts</h3><ul><li><strong>Pyruvate oxidation</strong> — pyruvate → acetyl-CoA.</li><li><strong>Krebs cycle</strong> — also called the citric acid cycle or TCA cycle.</li><li><strong>Location</strong> — mitochondrial matrix.</li></ul><h3>📚 Lesson Content</h3><h4>A. Pyruvate Oxidation</h4><p>Each pyruvate (3C) loses CO₂ and becomes acetyl-CoA (2C). Produces 1 NADH per pyruvate.</p><h4>B. Krebs Cycle (per acetyl-CoA)</h4><table><thead><tr><th>Product</th><th>Count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CO₂</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>NADH</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>FADH₂</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>ATP</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>C. Per Glucose (2 turns)</h4><ul><li>6 NADH</li><li>2 FADH₂</li><li>2 ATP</li><li>4 CO₂</li></ul>",
      "activity1": {
