    {
      "n": 4,
      "title": "Electron Transport Chain & Chemiosmosis",
      "competency": "STEM_BIO11/12-IIa-j-8",
      "content": "<h3>🎯 Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Describe the electron transport chain.</li><li>Explain chemiosmosis.</li><li>Explain the role of oxygen.</li><li>Summarize the ATP yield of respiration.</li></ul><h3>🔑 Key Concepts</h3><ul><li><strong>Electron transport chain (ETC)</strong> — series of protein complexes in the inner mitochondrial membrane.</li><li><strong>Chemiosmosis</strong> — ATP production via H⁺ gradient.</li><li><strong>ATP synthase</strong> — enzyme that produces ATP.</li><li><strong>Oxygen</strong> — final electron acceptor; forms water.</li></ul><h3>📚 Lesson Content</h3><h4>A. The ETC</h4><p>NADH and FADH₂ drop electrons at protein complexes. Electrons move through the chain, releasing energy that pumps H⁺ into the intermembrane space.</p><h4>B. Chemiosmosis</h4><p>H⁺ flows back through ATP synthase into the matrix, driving ATP production (~26–28 ATP).</p><h4>C. Oxygen's Role</h4><p>O₂ is the final electron acceptor. It combines with electrons and H⁺ to form H₂O. Without O₂, the ETC backs up.</p><h4>D. Total ATP Yield (Aerobic)</h4><ul><li>Glycolysis: 2 ATP</li><li>Krebs: 2 ATP</li><li>ETC: ~32–34 ATP</li><li><strong>Total: ~36–38 ATP</strong></li></ul>",
      "activity1": {
        "timeLimit": 90,
